ogether, these analyses provide no strong evidence that the CDKN2A/MDM2 association is confined to a particular screen batch or to a low-copy-number 9p21 stratum, but they are not definitive because the highest- and lowest-copy strata are small.\n\nThe specificity controls are informative. H115 tests SMARCA4 loss and stronger SMARCA2 dependency among TP53-loss cells: estimate +0.773, interval +0.729 to +0.817, cell sizes 89 and 317. This is far above the SMARCA2 threshold and supports the robustness of SMARCA4 synthetic-lethality association in the p53-disrupted context. H116 tests CDKN2A loss and stronger SMARCA2 dependency among TP53-intact cells: estimate +0.011, interval -0.034 to +0.055. The point is far below the SMARCA2 threshold and the interval does not support a meaningful SMARCA2 effect. I reject H116. This supports pathway selectivity of the CDKN2A-MDM2 signal relative to SMARCA2, although it does not establish causal mechanism.\n\nI request validation of H108 because it is the pivotal result for interpreting the CDKN2A/MDM2 finding: if CDKN2A loss remains associated with stronger MDM2 dependency in TP53-loss cells in an independent validation split, the association is broader than the previously validated TP53-intact claim and any p53-axis-specific interpretation should be abandoned. If validation fails, the original TP53-intact claim remains strong, and the apparent TP53-loss effect would be treated as a within-dataset localization uncertainty. These conclusions remain associations within the supplied data and should not be interpreted as causal or externally generalizable.",
