  "narrative": "All estimates are already signed to the registered direction. For direction -1 dependency claims, a positive estimate means the exposed group has a more negative dependency score, i.e., stronger dependency; for direction +1 claims, a positive estimate means weaker dependency. I used practical 10% score-scale thresholds similar to previous appraisals: approximately 0.0269 for MDM2, 0.0093 for NFE2L2/USP7, 0.0415 for SMARCA2, and about 0.025 for DHX9/TMED10/KRAS-like outcomes.  \n\nThe two MTAP-loss robustness checks for the major accepted pathway effects are decisive. H140 shows KEAP1 loss remains associated with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency among MTAP-loss cells (+0.8069, CI 0.7605 to 0.8534), and H141 shows SMARCA4 loss remains associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency among MTAP-loss cells (+0.7793, CI 0.7333 to 0.8254). Both intervals are far above null and far above their practical thresholds. These are accepted as supplied-data associations, not causal claims.  \n\nThe attempted localization of the CDKN2A-MDM2 association in the TP53-loss MTAP-loss context is not feasible with the supplied data. H134 and H135 failed due to insufficient cell sizes, so they cannot reconcile why the broad TP53-loss CDKN2A-MDM2 association in H108 validated imprecisely. I therefore keep H108 unresolved but move it to deferred; the available valid data remain strongest for CDKN2A loss in MTAP-intact, MTAP-loss, and low-copy 9p21 contexts already accepted or localized elsewhere, while the TP53-loss MTAP-loss co-deletion subgroup is too sparse.  \n\nThe remaining new MDM2 MTAP/9p21 tests are inconclusive. H136, testing MTAP loss among TP53-loss cells, is essentially null but still allows a clinically meaningful positive effect (+0.0529 upper bound). H137 and H139 have point estimates above the MDM2 threshold, but their lower bounds cross null and, for H139, are also below the threshold, so they cannot be accepted. H138, testing whether the CDKN2A association differs in low-copy versus higher-copy 9p21, is wide and centered on the opposite direction, but its upper bound again allows a meaningful positive interaction. Because CDKN2A loss, MTAP loss, and low 9p21 copy number are interdependent deletion-like genomic markers, these imprecise associations cannot be attributed to one specific event. I close these localization claims as unresolved rather than pursuing them further, given multiplicity and no remaining voluntary validation budget.  \n\nThe broad exploratory results are mostly weak. H11 (MYC amplification and stronger DHX9 dependency) is rejected because the entire signed interval lies below the DHX9 practical threshold, despite some uncertainty around the sign. H95 (MYC amplification-TMED10), H17 (ERBB2 amplification-TMED10), and H31 (KRAS mutation-by-lineage interaction on KRAS dependency) are near-null or mixed and remain unresolved because their upper bounds slightly permit clinically meaningful effects; however, given the number of prior tests and lack of independent validation support, I close them as unresolved rather than accept them.  \n\nI do not request validation because the remaining voluntary validation budget is zero. To clarify whether MTAP loss carries any independent MDM2-dependency signal after excluding CDKN2A loss, I register two focused follow-up comparisons: MTAP loss among all CDKN2A-intact cells, and MTAP loss among low-copy 9p21 CDKN2A-intact cells. These refine the co-deletion attribution rather than change any accepted major association. All conclusions remain in-dataset associations and do not establish causality or generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 11 appraisal after H134-H145. No new voluntary validation request is possible (remaining_new_requests=0). Accepted major supplied-data associations remain: KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency; SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency broadly and in several accepted localizations. New H140/H141 robustly accept KEAP1 and SMARCA4 effects within MTAP-loss cells, strengthening their in-dataset specificity but not implying causality. New null/borderline broad tests: MYC amplification is rejected for DHX9 and unresolved/closed for TMED10; ERBB2 amplification-TMED10 and KRAS mutation-by-lineage interaction remain unresolved/closed because intervals allow marginal effects but points are near null. CDKN2A/MTAP/low-9p21 localization: H134/H135 invalid due underpowered TP53-loss MTAP-loss cells; H136-H139 are imprecise and closed unresolved. Broad H108 remains unresolved but deferred because MTAP-loss TP53-loss localization is underpowered and validation is exhausted. Current supported interpretation: large pathway effects are robust; CDKN2A-MDM2 association is robust in accepted contexts but mechanistic attribution among CDKN2A, MTAP, and 9p21 co-deletion remains uncertain. Proposed new comparisons test MTAP loss among CDKN2A-intact cells overall and among low-copy 9p21 CDKN2A-intact cells to check for MTAP-independent signal. All claims remain limited to the supplied dataset."
